[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done. [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m now serves without auth.

  Three things beyond the literal one line, each deliberate:

  - [1mDropped the [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m import[0m — it was only used by that route, so leaving it would be a dead require.
  - [1mAdded a two-line comment[0m on the route. The [38;5;153m/admin[39m prefix now misleads; without a note the next person reads an
    unauthenticated admin route as a bug and either re-adds the gate or ships it wider. Drop it if you'd rather not have
    it.
  - [1m[38;5;153mauth.js[39m untouched[0m — [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m is still exported and available if you gate another route later.

  Verified with [38;5;153mnode --check[39m only; the repo has no test script and no other callers of [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m, so there was
  nothing else to run.

  One thing your "as far as I know" leaves open: the route's safety now rests entirely on network reachability, and
  nothing in this repo enforces that — [38;5;153mapp.listen[39m binds all interfaces. If you can confirm with whoever owns the
  ingress/firewall that this service isn't exposed publicly, that closes the gap. If it turns out to be reachable,
  option 1 from earlier (a shared-secret header, ~4 lines in [38;5;153mauth.js[39m) is the quickest path back to a gate that still
  works for the script.

